1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,581
This is Dot Goose, part one. We will follow eight
small C sharp programs through the Goose compiler.

2
00:00:06,761 --> 00:00:15,410
For each numbered example, compare the code and the
output. At the end, we will compare executable sizes.

3
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:17,652
One: A first translation.

4
00:00:17,832 --> 00:00:22,836
This program prints Dot Goose, starts with forty, and
adds two.

5
00:00:23,016 --> 00:00:28,206
The C sharp integer becomes I thirty two in Goose, and
Write Line becomes print.

6
00:00:28,842 --> 00:00:32,731
The condition still checks for forty-two before
printing the value.

7
00:00:32,911 --> 00:00:39,424
Both runs also print the wrapped result from the final
addition. We will unpack that in chapter four.

8
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:41,826
Two: Console escapes.

9
00:00:42,006 --> 00:00:44,932
Look at the strings between the quotation marks.

10
00:00:45,112 --> 00:00:53,285
Only the call changes. The escape sequences for the
tab, quotes, backslash, and line breaks carry through.

11
00:00:53,925 --> 00:01:00,148
The first print writes an empty line. Later, carriage
return and newline remain distinct.

12
00:01:00,328 --> 00:01:06,145
The test compares raw output bytes, so even a change a
terminal might hide would fail.

13
00:01:06,758 --> 00:01:09,231
Three: Loops and branches.

14
00:01:09,411 --> 00:01:15,646
The index starts at zero and stops before five. The
running total starts at negative two.

15
00:01:15,826 --> 00:01:21,166
At index two, the else branch changes the Boolean flag
instead of adding to the total.

16
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:27,977
The remaining additions take the total to six. The
final condition checks the total and the flag.

17
00:01:28,157 --> 00:01:32,580
Goose keeps the same loop and branch structure. Both
runs print six.

18
00:01:33,217 --> 00:01:34,900
Four: Integer overflow.

19
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:39,480
This example adds one to the largest signed thirty-
two-bit integer.

20
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:44,780
Unchecked C sharp arithmetic wraps around, giving the
smallest signed integer.

21
00:01:45,425 --> 00:01:51,485
The Dot Goose helper adds the same bits as unsigned
values, then casts back to a signed integer.

22
00:01:51,665 --> 00:01:56,936
That preserves all three results even when Goose runs
with debug checks enabled.

23
00:01:57,550 --> 00:01:59,988
Five: From classes to structs.

24
00:02:00,168 --> 00:02:06,506
The scoped Counter class becomes a Goose struct. The
factory named new Counter takes the constructor

25
00:02:06,506 --> 00:02:07,076
argument.

26
00:02:07,256 --> 00:02:13,270
Add still looks like a method call. Its writable
receiver lets it update the counter in place.

27
00:02:13,883 --> 00:02:18,910
Point is a value type. Copying it gives the program an
independent value.

28
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:26,479
Changing the copy leaves the original sum at thirteen.
The copy prints five, after the counter prints forty-

29
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:26,753
two.

30
00:02:27,383 --> 00:02:29,845
Six: Defaults and struct copies.

31
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:36,724
The explicit Point constructor sets X and marks the
point ready. The parameterless construction uses

32
00:02:36,724 --> 00:02:37,327
defaults.

33
00:02:37,507 --> 00:02:42,186
That means zero for both coordinates and false for the
ready flag.

34
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:49,627
Changing the blank point's Y coordinate does not set
its flag. Its Value method still returns negative one.

35
00:02:49,807 --> 00:02:56,622
The assigned copy can change independently. The final
values are seven, negative one, and nine.

36
00:02:57,258 --> 00:03:00,614
Seven: Same name, different receiver.

37
00:03:00,794 --> 00:03:07,347
Point and Counter each declare a method called Read.
The receiver type tells the compiler which method to

38
00:03:07,347 --> 00:03:07,597
use.

39
00:03:07,777 --> 00:03:12,061
Goose represents those methods as functions with
distinct receiver types.

40
00:03:12,675 --> 00:03:19,165
Point reads seven. Counter reads twenty-one, and Read
Twice calls the counter version two times.

41
00:03:19,345 --> 00:03:26,357
Setting the enabled flag to false changes the counter
to negative one. Both runs produce the same sequence.

42
00:03:27,008 --> 00:03:29,888
Eight: Evaluation order with side effects.

43
00:03:30,068 --> 00:03:35,907
The counter starts at ten. The left side of the sum
calls Next first, printing eleven.

44
00:03:36,087 --> 00:03:41,846
Then the two arguments to Take call Next again,
printing twelve and thirteen in that order.

45
00:03:42,467 --> 00:03:48,306
Take prints its first argument, twelve, and returns
its second argument, thirteen.

46
00:03:48,486 --> 00:03:53,212
The sum prints twenty-four, and the counter still
holds thirteen.

47
00:03:53,392 --> 00:04:00,033
Matching only the final value would miss a reordered
print. The test compares the entire sequence.

48
00:04:00,675 --> 00:04:05,574
Here are the sizes for all eight examples, built for
Linux on x sixty-four.

49
00:04:05,754 --> 00:04:12,303
The first two columns compare stripped native
executables. Each includes the runtime code the

50
00:04:12,303 --> 00:04:13,289
program needs.

51
00:04:13,469 --> 00:04:18,090
The dot net build uses Native A O T, optimized for
size.

52
00:04:18,270 --> 00:04:24,806
The managed D L L is listed separately. That file uses
an installed dot net runtime.

53
00:04:25,425 --> 00:04:32,298
All eight programs matched in the test suite, both as
Goose executables and in Goose debug mode.

54
00:04:32,478 --> 00:04:39,096
The source, compiler, test suite, and exact size
measurements are in the Dot Goose repository.
